  "audit" = {
      "label" = "R517 DSG underground waterways audit",
      "comment" = "The DSG underground waterways pins the standing water rules (the four forms of water underground, the low ceiling making normal boat travel impossible, the absence of wind making sailing virtually unheard-of except under ample ventilation, the practically nonexistent waves with the cave-in and earthquake tsunami class and the confining-wall chop lasting many turns, the fresh-water lakes and pools almost always safe to drink when fed by a stream or spring, and the reservoir whose dam may sit at the bottom like a cork in a leaky bucket), the deep mineral-heavy salt seas with their cold and geothermal-steam extremes, the flowing-water measurements (the impassable under 3 feet, narrow under 20 wide and 3 deep, average 20-60 wide and 4-8 deep, wide over 60 and deeper than 8, the canals and aqueducts and water wheels and locks, the 50% half-full and 100% unboatable flow percentages, and the 0-40 rate scale with the 20-feet-per-round multiplier), the six flow bands (stagnant 0, placid 1-2 at 40 feet or less per round with the 10-15-feet-per-mile drop, medium 2-6 at 40-120 feet per round with up to 30 feet per mile and the 1d6-per-mile roll triggering a 1d3-foot drop-off on a 5 or 6, rapids 6-12 at more than 120 up to 240 feet per round with the 30-200 slope and 1,000-foot audibility and the 1d6 roll where 6 means none and 1-5 counts 1d4-foot drop-offs, cascades 12-30 audible a mile away at 240-600 feet per round with the 200-1,000 slope and the 3d6-per-mile count of 1d8-foot drop-offs, and waterfalls 30-40 at at least 600 feet per round wherever the plunge exceeds 1,000 feet per mile, with boats uncontrollable and survival luck), the damming rules (the immediate backup flooding against three-dimensional maps, the 1,000-cubic-foot cube per flow point per turn doubled for wide and quartered for narrow waterways, the diversion down the path of least resistance, the 1d6 dam points per worker per turn against the flow-rate destruction, the 50/150/250 completed CPVs per the DMG pages 109-110 seam, and the half-flow leak destruction until washed away or repaired), the water temperature ladder (the almost universally cold water, the 1d4-mile warmth retention from surface sources, the geothermal warming, the 6d6 near-boiling burn per round with no save and the -2 per die minimum 1 under magical protection, the proportional partial-immersion damage, the 1d6 very-hot damage that spares the partially exposed and the magically protected, and the bath-warm immunity), and the access rules (the occasional direct surface embark, the usual dungeon-and-cavern route, the upstream-travel cost of a return-to-surface outlet, the sheer-cliff lake shores with their creature-built docks and wharves, and the smooth sandy banks of streams and rivers).",
